NEVER-SEEN BEATLES VIDEO CLIP RELEASED
By Linda Galeazzi (Editor-In-Chief)

October 22, 1999.  Rare, never-before-seen videotape of the Beatles
singing a song called "Hey Bulldog" has been released by Apple Corps. in
the hopes of re-kindling Beatlemania and encouraging a whole new
generation of listeners into purchasing the band's albums.  The video
footage shows John, Paul, George and Ringo in the legendary Abbey Road
Studios, working on the song while Yoko Ono and the late Linda McCartney
can be seen in the background, trying to stab each other with butcher
knives.

"I think it makes a cool video," Sir Paul McCartney told the press.
"It's very of the time."

An Apple Corp. insider tells CyberStones that the company is hoping to
lure younger audiences who weren't even alive when the Beatles were
around into buying Beatles records and re-discovering the legendary
band.

"Little Suzi and little Timmy have never heard the original versions of
'Love Me Do,' 'Let it Be' and 'Hey Jude,'" says the insider, "and we
think this is something that needs to be rectified.  We want to make
sure everyone born after 1980 knows the Beatles as the most innovative,
revolutionary, ground-breaking band of the 20th Century and NOT as
'those guys who wrote all that farkin' Muzak.'"

McCartney chuckled, "I like the moment when I'm harmonizing with John
(Lennon) and he says something about a dog and I just started barking, I
started being a dog."

Hm.  Yeah.  I liked that part, too.  Found it danged arousing, too, for
some unknown reason.

Anyway, CyberStones saw the footage.  CyberStones gives it a
thumbs-down.  CyberStones encourages everyone to listen to "Ob-La-Di
Ob-La-Da" to nip this latest attempt at rekindling Beatlemania in the
bud.

